/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A lowpass signal m(t) is bandlimited to fm hertz when its spectrum M(f) is zero for |f| > fm.
   - Ideal sampling multiplies m(t) by an impulse train Σ δ(t − nTs).
   - S(f) is the spectrum of the sampled signal s(t) = Σ m(nTs) δ(t − nTs); k runs over all integers.
   - The copy at 0 ends at fm; the copy at fs starts at fs − fm.
   - A tone at f0 above fs/2 does not vanish.
   - With fs = 7 kHz, a 1 kHz tone stays at 1 kHz, but a 4 kHz tone is above 3.5 kHz and folds to |4 − 7| = 3 kHz.
   - So aliasing is stopped before the sampler.
   - Ideal reconstruction is sinc interpolation: m(t) = Σ m(nTs) sinc((t − nTs)/Ts), with sinc(x) = sin(πx)/(πx).
   - In natural sampling the pulse top follows m(t); in flat-top sampling a sample-and-hold keeps the top at m(nTs) for a width τ ≤ Ts.
   - A band-pass signal of bandwidth B = fH − fL can be sampled below 2fH if the shifted copies interleave.
*/
