/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a pulse train with period Ts = 1/fs, pulse width τ and duty cycle d = τ/Ts, with fs ≥ 2fm.
   - Flat-top PAM is the sampled message filtered by one rectangular pulse, so its spectrum is
   - P(f) = fs τ sinc(fτ) Σ M(f − kfs)
   - Each copy of M(f), at 0, ±fs, …, is shaped by τ sinc(fτ), which has its first null at 1/τ.
   - The receiver lowpass filters at fm; for flat-top PAM it adds a 1/sinc equalizer if τ is not small.
   - Pulse width modulation (PWM), also called pulse duration modulation (PDM), keeps the height fixed and sets the width τn = τ0 + kw m(nTs), where τ0 is the unmodulated width and kw the width sensitivity in s/V.
   - Pulse position modulation (PPM) keeps height and width fixed and shifts the whole pulse from its nominal position by kp m(nTs).
   - The bandwidth of a pulse train is set by its fastest feature.
   - With constant height, a receiver can slice the pulses at half height and ignore most amplitude noise; noise then matters only through the timing jitter it causes on a sloping edge.
*/
